.Dd May 29, 2026
.Dt CONVERT-TO-JSON 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm convert-to-json
.Nd convert simple object-storage formats to JSON
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl f
.Ar file ...
.Nm
.Fl t Ar type
.Op Fl f
.Op Fl o Ar output.json
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a pure Bash converter for creating JSON from CSV, SQL-style CSV rows,
simple YAML, and simple TOML.
.Pp
When file paths are provided,
.Nm
creates a JSON file next to each source file by replacing the source extension
with
.Pa .json .
For example,
.Pa data.csv
is written as
.Pa data.json .
.Pp
When input is piped on standard input,
.Nm
requires an explicit input type with
.Fl t
or
.Fl -type .
Piped output is written to standard output unless
.Fl o
is provided.
.Pp
This program intentionally does not use external JSON or document converters.
Because it is pure Bash, YAML and TOML support is conservative and limited to
simple, predictable data.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl f , Fl -force
Overwrite an existing JSON output file.
.Pp
Without this option,
.Nm
refuses to replace an existing output file.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Show usage information and exit.
.It Fl o Ar file , Fl -output Ar file
Write piped conversion output to
.Ar file
instead of standard output.
.Pp
This option is only valid for piped input.
.It Fl t Ar type , Fl -type Ar type , Fl -format Ar type
Specify the input type for piped content.
.Pp
Supported values are
.Cm csv ,
.Cm sql ,
.Cm yaml ,
.Cm yml ,
and
.Cm toml .
.El
.Sh SUPPORTED FORMATS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm csv
Converts comma-separated rows to an array of JSON objects.
The first row is used as object keys.
.Pp
Quoted fields, commas inside quoted fields, and escaped double quotes are
supported.
Multiline quoted fields are not supported.
.It Cm sql
Converts SELECT-like CSV output to an array of JSON objects.
The first row is used as object keys.
.Pp
.Nm
does not parse SQL statements.
.It Cm yaml , Cm yml
Converts simple YAML mappings to JSON objects.
.Pp
Supported YAML includes scalar key/value pairs, nested mappings using two-space
indentation, inline arrays, and scalar list items under a mapping key.
.It Cm toml
Converts simple TOML to JSON objects.
.Pp
Supported TOML includes scalar key/value pairs, dotted sections, and inline
arrays.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Pp
The command exits with a non-zero status if any input file cannot be converted,
if an output file already exists without
.Fl f ,
if a required option value is missing, or if the input format is unsupported.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Convert local files and write JSON files next to them:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\&./convert-to-json.sh data.csv config.yaml settings.toml rows.sql
.Ed
.Pp
Overwrite existing JSON outputs:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\&./convert-to-json.sh -f data.csv
.Ed
.Pp
Convert CSV downloaded from the web and write to standard output:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
curl -L "https://example.com/data.csv" | \&./convert-to-json.sh --type csv
.Ed
.Pp
Convert TOML from standard input and save it to a file:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
curl -L "https://example.com/settings.toml" | \&./convert-to-json.sh --type toml -o settings.json
.Ed
.Pp
Force overwrite for piped output:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
curl -L "https://example.com/data.csv" | \&./convert-to-json.sh --type csv -f -o data.json
.Ed
.Sh LIMITATIONS
.Nm
is intentionally conservative.
It is not a complete CSV, SQL, YAML, or TOML parser.
.Pp
CSV conversion does not support multiline quoted fields.
.Pp
YAML conversion does not support anchors, aliases, tags, block scalars, flow
objects, top-level arrays, arrays of objects, or advanced typing.
.Pp
TOML conversion does not support multiline strings, arrays of tables, quoted
dotted keys, dates, or inline tables.
.Pp
For full-spec conversion, use a dedicated parser or converter.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr bash 1 ,
.Xr jq 1
